From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: media: cedrus: drive the VE decode-time scale-down

The H713 display pipeline has no downscaler -- an exhaustive census of the MIPS
display firmware found three ratio-carrying blocks and none can do 1920x1080 ->
1280x720. Static RE of the vendor Android stack says why: the vendor does not
downscale on the display side at all. It downscales AT THE DECODER.

The vendor's sd_rotate_ctrl_reg40 / sd_rotate_buf_addr_reg44 /
sd_rotate_chroma_buf_addr_reg48 are offsets within the H.264 ENGINE block, not
the top-level VE register file: mainline already names the first
VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL (0x240 = VE_ENGINE_DEC_H264 + 0x40) and leaves 0x244/0x248 as
an unnamed gap, and the H.265 engine carries the same trio as
VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_{CTRL,LUMA_ADDR,CHROMA_ADDR}. This patch names the two H.264
address registers and drives all three.

Confirmed on the board. The control word decodes as two independent 2-bit
fields:

    VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL[9:8]    horizontal   0 = 1:1, 1 = 1/2, 2 = 1/4
    VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL[11:10]  vertical     0 = 1:1, 1 = 1/2, 2 = 1/4
    3 in either field produces a partial, malformed write.

Twelve control words were swept against a 1920x1088 source and every output size
matched that model exactly, including two nulls and two invalid-field partials;
960x544, 1920x544 and 960x1088 were rendered and visually confirmed correctly
proportioned. So the scale-down is power-of-two per axis -- matching
H264ComputeScaleRatio, which returns 0/1/2 for none/half/quarter -- and it cannot
produce 1280x720. The nearest useful output is 960x544.

Three things had to be right before any of this produced a picture, and each
failed silently first:

  - The registers must be programmed AFTER the codec's own setup, where
    cedrus_engine_enable() writes VE_MODE. A disabled engine accepts the control
    write and drops the addresses, which read back zero. Hence sd_stage.
  - The addresses go in raw and unshifted, unlike several other engine
    registers. Hence sd_shift, and the readback that settles it.
  - The secondary output format defaults to TILED_32_NV12. Linear NV12 needs the
    EXT table (VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN[31:30] = 1) and VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT[3:0] = 4;
    clearing those two bits selects the tiled default, which yields a striped
    dump from a scaler that is working correctly. Hence sd_fmt.

The arbitrary-ratio path was also probed and is NOT reachable from our side.
H264ConfigNewScaler genuinely computes a non-power-of-two ratio -- vdiv.f32 then
a 12-bit fixed-point conversion -- and writes top-level 0xcc/0xe8/0xec plus
engine 0x220/0x244/0x248. Its size and ratio registers, however, are at
getRegBase(7) + 0x10/0x14/0x18/0xe4/0xf8/0xfc, and register block 7 is not in
the VE's 4 KiB MMIO window: the whole range 0x300..0xfff reads zero during an
active decode, and /proc/iomem has no other VE-adjacent region. Top-level 0xcc is
reachable and is the secondary LINE STRIDE, confirmed by writing 1280 and seeing
a 960-wide image at a 1280 pitch with untouched poison in the trailing 320
columns. VE_H264_CTRL bit 11, the one explicit set-bit in the vendor sequence,
times out the decode rather than enabling anything.

So this is a harness, not an implementation. Every unknown is a module parameter,
and it is inert unless sd_w and sd_h are both set -- with the defaults not a
single register write changes:

    sunxi_cedrus.sd_w=1920 sd_h=2176    target size; 0 (default) disables all
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_ctrl=0x500          raw VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_fmt=1               linear NV12 rather than tiled
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_stage=1             program after the codec setup
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_shift=0             address shift
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_poke="0xcc=1280"    arbitrary off=val register writes

Two debugfs nodes expose the results. /sys/kernel/debug/cedrus_sd_buf is the
secondary output buffer, re-poisoned with 0xa5 whenever sd_ctrl changes so each
candidate is judged against a clean buffer and "wrote nothing" stays
distinguishable from "wrote black"; a shadow copy is taken when the decoding
context is released so the result outlives the player that produced it.
/sys/kernel/debug/cedrus_sd_regs is a snapshot of the whole 4 KiB register file
taken at the decode trigger -- reading the VE from userspace instead is
unreliable, because a runtime-suspended VE reads zero everywhere and that is
indistinguishable from a block that is not there.

The SRAM access ports (0x1e0/0x1e4, 0x2e0/0x2e4, 0x5e0/0x5e4) are excluded from
both the snapshot and the poke list: they walk an internal pointer, so touching
them would corrupt the decode rather than observe it.

Driven by tools/video/ve-scaledown-sweep.sh and
tools/video/ve-newscaler-probe.sh.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---

diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c
@@ -410,6 +410,9 @@
 	v4l2_ctrl_handler_free(&ctx->hdl);
 	kfree(ctx->ctrls);
 
+	/* EXPERIMENT: release the scale-down secondary output buffer. */
+	cedrus_sd_free(ctx);
+
 	v4l2_fh_exit(&ctx->fh);
 
 	kfree(ctx);
diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
@@ -93,11 +93,20 @@
 	v4l2_m2m_buf_copy_metadata(run.src, run.dst, true);
 
 	cedrus_dst_format_set(dev, &ctx->dst_fmt);
+	/* EXPERIMENT: point the scale-down unit at our own buffer. */
+	cedrus_sd_setup(ctx, 0);
 
 	error = ctx->current_codec->setup(ctx, &run);
 	if (error)
 		v4l2_err(&ctx->dev->v4l2_dev,
 			 "Failed to setup decoding job: %d\n", error);
+	else
+		/*
+		 * The codec's setup runs cedrus_engine_enable(), so this is the
+		 * first point at which the engine is actually on -- which is
+		 * where the vendor programs scale/rotate.
+		 */
+		cedrus_sd_setup(ctx, 1);
 
 	/* Complete request(s) controls if needed. */
 
@@ -110,6 +119,9 @@
 		schedule_delayed_work(&dev->watchdog_work,
 				      msecs_to_jiffies(2000));
 
+		/* EXPERIMENT: did the scale-down values survive to the trigger? */
+		cedrus_sd_verify(ctx);
+
 		ctx->current_codec->trigger(ctx);
 	} else {
 		v4l2_m2m_buf_done_and_job_finish(ctx->dev->m2m_dev,
diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
@@ -121,6 +121,14 @@
 	struct cedrus_dec_ops		*current_codec;
 	unsigned int			bit_depth;
 
+	/*
+	 * EXPERIMENT: secondary (scale-down) output buffer. Allocated lazily
+	 * when sd_w/sd_h are set, freed with the context.
+	 */
+	void				*sd_buf;
+	dma_addr_t			sd_buf_dma;
+	size_t				sd_buf_size;
+
 	struct v4l2_ctrl_handler	hdl;
 	struct v4l2_ctrl		**ctrls;
 
diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
@@ -16,6 +16,10 @@
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_reserved_mem.h>
+#include <linux/debugfs.h>
+#include <linux/seq_file.h>
+#include <linux/ctype.h>
+#include <linux/vmalloc.h>
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
@@ -31,6 +35,417 @@
 #include "cedrus_hw.h"
 #include "cedrus_regs.h"
 
+/*
+ * EXPERIMENT: the VE scale-down secondary output.
+ *
+ * Inert unless sd_w and sd_h are both non-zero: with the defaults not one
+ * register write changes, so a kernel carrying this behaves exactly as before.
+ */
+static unsigned int sd_w;
+static unsigned int sd_h;
+static unsigned int sd_ctrl;
+static unsigned int sd_fmt;
+static unsigned int sd_stage = 1;
+static unsigned int sd_shift;
+module_param(sd_w, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_w, "EXPERIMENT: scale-down target width (0 = disabled)");
+module_param(sd_h, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_h, "EXPERIMENT: scale-down target height (0 = disabled)");
+module_param(sd_ctrl, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_ctrl,
+		 "EXPERIMENT: raw value for VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL (encoding unknown)");
+module_param(sd_fmt, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_fmt, "EXPERIMENT: also set the secondary output format bits");
+module_param(sd_stage, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_stage,
+		 "EXPERIMENT: program at 0 = before the codec setup, 1 = after it (default; engine enabled)");
+module_param(sd_shift, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_shift,
+		 "EXPERIMENT: right-shift applied to the output addresses (0 = raw, 8 = >>8)");
+
+/*
+ * A generic poke list, applied at the same point as the scale-down setup:
+ * "0xcc=1280,0xf8=0x123". The secondary output's geometry registers are not all
+ * named by mainline -- 0xe8 and 0xec provably carry secondary fields, so the
+ * unnamed 0xcc/0xe4/0xf8/0xfc around them are the candidates -- and a parameter
+ * beats a rebuild per candidate.
+ */
+static char *sd_poke;
+module_param(sd_poke, charp, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_poke,
+		 "EXPERIMENT: comma-separated off=val register writes, e.g. \"0xcc=1280\"");
+
+static struct dentry *cedrus_sd_debugfs;
+static struct cedrus_ctx *cedrus_sd_ctx;
+static unsigned int sd_ctrl_last;
+static bool sd_ctrl_armed;
+static bool sd_log_once;
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+/*
+ * A shadow copy of the last secondary output, taken when the decoding context
+ * goes away. Without it the result is only readable while a decode is in
+ * flight, because the DMA buffer belongs to the context: closing the decoder
+ * would destroy the very thing the experiment is trying to look at.
+ */
+static void *cedrus_sd_shadow;
+static size_t cedrus_sd_shadow_size;
+
+/*
+ * A snapshot of the VE register file taken at the moment the decode is
+ * triggered, i.e. with configuration complete and the engine enabled. Reading
+ * the VE from userspace instead is unreliable: runtime-suspended the whole
+ * window reads zero, which is indistinguishable from a block that is not
+ * there. Diffing a scaler-on snapshot against a scaler-off one is what
+ * identifies the geometry registers without guessing.
+ */
+#define CEDRUS_SD_REG_LEN	0x1000
+
+/*
+ * The SRAM access ports: writing the offset then reading or writing the data
+ * port walks an internal pointer, so touching them while sweeping would corrupt
+ * the decode rather than observe it. One pair per engine that has one.
+ */
+static bool cedrus_sd_reg_is_port(unsigned int off)
+{
+	return off == 0x1e0 || off == 0x1e4 ||	/* MPEG */
+	       off == 0x2e0 || off == 0x2e4 ||	/* AVC  */
+	       off == 0x5e0 || off == 0x5e4;	/* HEVC */
+}
+
+static u32 cedrus_sd_regs[CEDRUS_SD_REG_LEN / 4];
+static bool cedrus_sd_regs_valid;
+static struct dentry *cedrus_sd_regs_debugfs;
+
+static int cedrus_sd_regs_show(struct seq_file *s, void *unused)
+{
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	if (!cedrus_sd_regs_valid) {
+		seq_puts(s, "# no snapshot: run a decode with sd_w/sd_h set\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	seq_puts(s, "# VE register snapshot taken at decode trigger\n");
+	seq_puts(s, "# 0x2e0/0x2e4 are the AVC SRAM port and are NOT read:\n");
+	seq_puts(s, "# reading the data port auto-increments and would corrupt the decode\n");
+	for (i = 0; i < CEDRUS_SD_REG_LEN / 4; i++) {
+		if (cedrus_sd_reg_is_port(i * 4))
+			seq_printf(s, "%#05x skipped\n", i * 4);
+		else
+			seq_printf(s, "%#05x %#010x\n", i * 4, cedrus_sd_regs[i]);
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+DEFINE_SHOW_ATTRIBUTE(cedrus_sd_regs);
+
+/*
+ * Apply the poke list. Offsets are bounded to the register window, and the AVC
+ * SRAM ports are refused: writing the data port advances an internal pointer
+ * and would corrupt the decode rather than configure anything.
+ */
+static void cedrus_sd_apply_poke(struct cedrus_dev *dev, bool log)
+{
+	const char *p = sd_poke;
+	char tok[32];
+
+	if (!p || !*p)
+		return;
+
+	while (*p) {
+		unsigned int off, val;
+		const char *eq, *end;
+		size_t n;
+
+		/*
+		 * Skip separators and whitespace, so `echo "" > sd_poke` -- which
+		 * writes a newline rather than nothing -- clears the list instead
+		 * of logging a parse warning for it.
+		 */
+		while (*p == ',' || isspace(*p))
+			p++;
+		if (!*p)
+			break;
+
+		end = strchrnul(p, ',');
+		n = end - p;
+		if (n >= sizeof(tok)) {
+			dev_warn(dev->dev, "sd: poke token too long\n");
+			return;
+		}
+		memcpy(tok, p, n);
+		tok[n] = '\0';
+		p = end;
+
+		while (n && isspace(tok[n - 1]))
+			tok[--n] = '\0';
+		if (!n)
+			continue;
+
+		eq = strchr(tok, '=');
+		if (!eq) {
+			dev_warn(dev->dev, "sd: poke '%s' has no '='\n", tok);
+			continue;
+		}
+		((char *)eq)[0] = '\0';
+
+		if (kstrtouint(tok, 0, &off) || kstrtouint(eq + 1, 0, &val)) {
+			dev_warn(dev->dev, "sd: poke '%s' does not parse\n", tok);
+			continue;
+		}
+		if (off & 3 || off >= CEDRUS_SD_REG_LEN) {
+			dev_warn(dev->dev,
+				 "sd: poke offset %#x out of range or unaligned\n",
+				 off);
+			continue;
+		}
+		if (cedrus_sd_reg_is_port(off)) {
+			dev_warn(dev->dev,
+				 "sd: refusing to poke the SRAM port %#x\n", off);
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		cedrus_write(dev, off, val);
+		if (log)
+			dev_info(dev->dev, "sd: poke %#05x = %#010x (reads %#010x)\n",
+				 off, val, cedrus_read(dev, off));
+	}
+}
+
+static void cedrus_sd_snapshot(struct cedrus_dev *dev)
+{
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < CEDRUS_SD_REG_LEN / 4; i++) {
+		/* SRAM data ports auto-increment on read. Never touch them. */
+		if (cedrus_sd_reg_is_port(i * 4))
+			continue;
+		cedrus_sd_regs[i] = cedrus_read(dev, i * 4);
+	}
+	cedrus_sd_regs_valid = true;
+}
+
+static ssize_t cedrus_sd_read(struct file *file, char __user *buf,
+			      size_t count, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	struct cedrus_ctx *ctx;
+	ssize_t ret = 0;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+	ctx = cedrus_sd_ctx;
+	if (ctx && ctx->sd_buf)
+		ret = simple_read_from_buffer(buf, count, ppos, ctx->sd_buf,
+					      ctx->sd_buf_size);
+	else if (cedrus_sd_shadow)
+		ret = simple_read_from_buffer(buf, count, ppos,
+					      cedrus_sd_shadow,
+					      cedrus_sd_shadow_size);
+
+	mutex_unlock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations cedrus_sd_fops = {
+	.owner	= THIS_MODULE,
+	.read	= cedrus_sd_read,
+	.llseek	= default_llseek,
+};
+
+/*
+ * The node is owned by the driver, not by a decoding context: a debugfs file
+ * whose fops live in this module must not outlive the module, and one created
+ * per context would vanish the moment a player exits.
+ */
+void cedrus_sd_debugfs_init(void)
+{
+	cedrus_sd_debugfs = debugfs_create_file("cedrus_sd_buf", 0400, NULL,
+						NULL, &cedrus_sd_fops);
+	cedrus_sd_regs_debugfs = debugfs_create_file("cedrus_sd_regs", 0400, NULL,
+						     NULL,
+						     &cedrus_sd_regs_fops);
+}
+
+void cedrus_sd_debugfs_exit(void)
+{
+	debugfs_remove(cedrus_sd_debugfs);
+	cedrus_sd_debugfs = NULL;
+	debugfs_remove(cedrus_sd_regs_debugfs);
+	cedrus_sd_regs_debugfs = NULL;
+	cedrus_sd_regs_valid = false;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+	vfree(cedrus_sd_shadow);
+	cedrus_sd_shadow = NULL;
+	cedrus_sd_shadow_size = 0;
+	mutex_unlock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+}
+
+void cedrus_sd_free(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
+{
+	if (!ctx->sd_buf)
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+	if (cedrus_sd_ctx == ctx) {
+		if (cedrus_sd_shadow_size != ctx->sd_buf_size) {
+			vfree(cedrus_sd_shadow);
+			cedrus_sd_shadow = vmalloc(ctx->sd_buf_size);
+			cedrus_sd_shadow_size = cedrus_sd_shadow ?
+						ctx->sd_buf_size : 0;
+		}
+		if (cedrus_sd_shadow)
+			memcpy(cedrus_sd_shadow, ctx->sd_buf,
+			       cedrus_sd_shadow_size);
+
+		cedrus_sd_ctx = NULL;
+	}
+
+	mutex_unlock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+	dma_free_coherent(ctx->dev->dev, ctx->sd_buf_size, ctx->sd_buf,
+			  ctx->sd_buf_dma);
+	ctx->sd_buf = NULL;
+	ctx->sd_buf_size = 0;
+}
+
+void cedrus_sd_setup(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx, unsigned int stage)
+{
+	struct cedrus_dev *dev = ctx->dev;
+	unsigned int w = ALIGN(sd_w, 16);
+	unsigned int h = ALIGN(sd_h, 16);
+	size_t luma, size;
+	bool log = false;
+	u32 reg;
+
+	if (!sd_w || !sd_h)
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * Which side of the codec's own setup to program from. The vendor
+	 * configures scale/rotate as part of the decode configuration, i.e.
+	 * after cedrus_engine_enable() has written VE_MODE; programming a
+	 * disabled engine would be a null result that means nothing. Kept as a
+	 * parameter so both orderings can be compared without a rebuild.
+	 */
+	if (stage != sd_stage)
+		return;
+
+	luma = (size_t)w * h;
+	size = luma * 3 / 2;
+
+	if (ctx->sd_buf && ctx->sd_buf_size != size)
+		cedrus_sd_free(ctx);
+
+	if (!ctx->sd_buf) {
+		ctx->sd_buf = dma_alloc_coherent(dev->dev, size,
+						 &ctx->sd_buf_dma, GFP_KERNEL);
+		if (!ctx->sd_buf) {
+			dev_warn(dev->dev, "sd: cannot allocate %zu bytes\n",
+				 size);
+			return;
+		}
+		ctx->sd_buf_size = size;
+		mutex_lock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+		cedrus_sd_ctx = ctx;
+		mutex_unlock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+		sd_ctrl_armed = false;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Re-poison whenever the swept value changes, so each candidate is
+	 * judged against a clean buffer rather than against whatever the
+	 * previous candidate left behind. A scaler that never runs leaves the
+	 * pattern intact, which separates "wrote nothing" from "wrote black" --
+	 * a flat fill is ambiguous on this hardware. Logging here rather than
+	 * at allocation means one line per swept value, not one per decode.
+	 */
+	if (!sd_ctrl_armed || sd_ctrl != sd_ctrl_last) {
+		memset(ctx->sd_buf, 0xa5, ctx->sd_buf_size);
+		sd_ctrl_last = sd_ctrl;
+		sd_ctrl_armed = true;
+		log = true;
+		sd_log_once = true;
+		dev_info(dev->dev,
+			 "sd: %ux%u buffer %zu bytes at %pad, ctrl %#x, stage %u\n",
+			 w, h, size, &ctx->sd_buf_dma, sd_ctrl, sd_stage);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * H.264 frame addresses go into the engine raw (see cedrus_buf_addr),
+	 * but several other engine registers take them >> 8. Rather than guess,
+	 * write under sd_shift and read back: a register that stores 0xff000000
+	 * as 0x00ff0000 has told us its own convention.
+	 */
+	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_LUMA_ADDR,
+		     (u32)(ctx->sd_buf_dma >> sd_shift));
+	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CHROMA_ADDR,
+		     (u32)((ctx->sd_buf_dma + luma) >> sd_shift));
+
+	if (sd_fmt) {
+		/*
+		 * The secondary output format is a two-level selector: the top
+		 * two bits of VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN pick the table, and only the
+		 * EXT table carries linear NV12, in VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT[3:0].
+		 * Clearing those two bits selects TILED_32_NV12 (the reset
+		 * default), which is what produced a striped dump on the first
+		 * hit -- the scaler was running correctly and being read wrong.
+		 */
+		reg = cedrus_read(dev, VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN);
+		reg = (reg & ~(0x3u << 30)) | VE_SECONDARY_OUT_FMT_EXT;
+		cedrus_write(dev, VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN, reg);
+
+		reg = cedrus_read(dev, VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT);
+		reg = (reg & ~0xfu) | VE_SECONDARY_OUT_FMT_EXT_NV12;
+		cedrus_write(dev, VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT, reg);
+	}
+
+	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL, sd_ctrl);
+
+	cedrus_sd_apply_poke(dev, log);
+
+	if (log) {
+		/*
+		 * Read the registers back. A null result from a write that never
+		 * landed is indistinguishable from a null result from a scaler
+		 * that does not exist -- and only one of those is a finding.
+		 */
+		dev_info(dev->dev,
+			 "sd: wrote  ctrl=%#010x luma=%#010x chroma=%#010x  VE_MODE=%#010x\n",
+			 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL),
+			 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_LUMA_ADDR),
+			 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CHROMA_ADDR),
+			 cedrus_read(dev, VE_MODE));
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Read the scale-down registers back at the point the decode is actually
+ * triggered, which is what says whether the values survived the codec's own
+ * setup -- cedrus_engine_enable() rewrites VE_MODE, and the engine is disabled
+ * between frames.
+ */
